Feature: Backup and Clean-up of Customer 360 Raw Table in Databricks

  The PySpark backup job shall export all records from the purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw table
  as compressed Parquet files partitioned by the `state` column, storing them in the Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup Databricks volume.
  After a successful backup, the job shall execute a Databricks vacuum operation to remove records older than 30 days,
  based on the `updated_at` timestamp column, retaining only the most recent 30 days (720 hours) of records.

  Background:
    Given the Unity Catalog table is purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw
    And the backup volume is at Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup
    And the table schema includes columns: customer_id (string), name (string), email (string), state (string), updated_at (timestamp), created_at (timestamp), status (string)
    And "state" is defined as the two-letter US state abbreviation found in the "state" column
    And all columns are to be included in the backup
    And the parquet files must use "snappy" compression
    And the backup job is triggered on demand
    And the vacuum operation uses the "updated_at" column to determine record age
    And the minimum retention for backup files is 90 days
    And user "svc_data_backup" has read/write access to both Unity Catalog table and backup volume

  Scenario: Successful backup and vacuum operation with data present
    Given the customer_360_raw table contains records with various states and updated_at timestamps within the last 90 days
    When the backup PySpark script is executed
    Then Parquet files are written to Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup
    And all columns are present in the Parquet files
    And each Parquet file is partitioned by the "state" column
    And all Parquet files use "snappy" compression
    And the quantity of rows in the backup equals the row count of the source table at time of backup
    And the vacuum operation deletes records from purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw where updated_at < (current timestamp - 30 days)
    And only records with updated_at >= (current timestamp - 30 days) are retained in the table

  Scenario: Backup when source table is empty
    Given the customer_360_raw table contains zero records
    When the backup PySpark script is executed
    Then empty Parquet partitions are created (or none are created) in Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup
    And there are no rows in the resulting Parquet files
    And the vacuum operation completes without errors

  Scenario: State partitioning with bad or null state values
    Given the customer_360_raw table contains records with state values: 'NY', 'CA', '', null, 'ZZ'
    When the backup PySpark script is executed
    Then records with valid 2-letter US state codes are partitioned by their state
    And records where state is empty or null are placed in a partition named "unknown"
    And records with non-standard state codes (e.g., 'ZZ') are placed in a partition named "other"

  Scenario: Data quality - invalid updated_at values
    Given the customer_360_raw table contains rows where updated_at is null or in the future
    When the backup PySpark script is executed
    Then records with null updated_at are included in the backup but are not vacuumed
    And records where updated_at > current timestamp are preserved in both backup and source (not vacuumed)

  Scenario: Vacuum operation retention enforcement
    Given the customer_360_raw table has 100,000 records and 30,000 are older than 30 days (updated_at < current timestamp - 30 days)
    When the backup PySpark script is executed
    Then only 70,000 records are retained in the source table post-vacuum
    And all 100,000 records are present in the backup Parquet files

  Scenario: Permissions error when accessing source table
    Given the service account lacks SELECT privilege on purgo_databricks.purgo_playground.customer_360_raw
    When the backup PySpark script is executed
    Then the job fails
    And the error message contains "Permission denied: unable to read source table"

  Scenario: Permissions error when writing to backup volume
    Given the service account lacks WRITE privileges on Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup
    When the backup PySpark script is executed
    Then the job fails
    And the error message contains "Permission denied: unable to write to backup volume"

  Scenario Outline: Validation of Parquet file output
    Given the backup has completed successfully
    When I examine the Parquet files for <State>
    Then there is a partition folder Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup/state=<State>
    And the number of rows in the partition equals the row count in the source table where state = <State>
    And the Parquet schema matches: customer_id (string), name (string), email (string), state (string), updated_at (timestamp), created_at (timestamp), status (string)
    And compression codec for all Parquet files is "snappy"

    Examples:
      | State | 
      | NY    | 
      | CA    |
      | TX    |
      | unknown |
      | other   |

  Scenario: Backup retention policy enforcement
    Given Parquet backup files in Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup are older than 90 days
    When the backup retention job runs
    Then backup files older than 90 days are deleted
    And backup files 90 days old or newer are retained

  Scenario Outline: Error handling for invalid data types in columns
    Given the customer_360_raw table contains a record with <InvalidValue> in <Column>
    When the backup PySpark script is executed
    Then the job logs a warning "Invalid data type in <Column> for record with customer_id=<CustomerId>"
    And the record is still included in the backup

    Examples:
      | Column      | InvalidValue         | CustomerId   |
      | updated_at  | "not_a_timestamp"    | cust100      |
      | state       | 12345                | cust200      |
      | email       | true                 | cust300      |

  Scenario: Error scenario - insufficient storage in backup volume
    Given Volumes/customer_360_raw_backup is nearly full
    When the backup PySpark script is executed
    Then the job terminates with error "Insufficient storage space in backup volume"

  Scenario: Error scenario - failed vacuum
    Given the Databricks vacuum command fails with error "Databricks timeout"
    When the backup PySpark script is executed
    Then the job status is "partial_failure"
    And a message is logged "Backup completed; vacuum failed: Databricks timeout"
